 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A seed is a fertilised ovule. It contains a young plant (the embryo) and a store of food, inside a protective coat.\nStructure of a bean seed:\n• testa (seed coat): protects the seed;\n• hilum: the scar where the seed was attached to the pod;\n• micropyle: a tiny hole near the hilum that lets in water;\n• two cotyledons: seed leaves that store food;\n• embryo: the plumule (grows into the shoot) and the radicle (grows into the root).\nBean, groundnut: two cotyledons (dicotyledons). Maize: one cotyledon (monocotyledon), with food stored in the endosperm.\nImportance of seeds: they grow into new plants; they can wait (stay dormant) until conditions are good; they are food for people and animals (maize, beans, groundnuts) and give oils (palm kernel, groundnut).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "k": 321
  },
  "problem": "Ayuk soaks a bean seed and opens it. Which parts form the embryo, and what does each become?",
  "steps": [
   "The embryo is the young plant between the cotyledons.",
   "The plumule ({bioLetter(k, 6, 4)} in the drawing) grows into the shoot; the radicle ({bioLetter(k, 6, 5)}) grows into the root."
  ],
  "answer": "The plumule (shoot) and the radicle (root).",
  "checks": [],
  "figure": "beanSeed(k)"
 },
